      c("A diagnostic result needs the right context", '<p><strong>Random plasma glucose ≥200 mg/dL with classic hyperglycemic symptoms or crisis</strong> can diagnose diabetes.</p><p>Without unequivocal hyperglycemia, <strong>confirm with repeat testing</strong>. A1C reflects roughly <strong>2–3 months</strong>, weighted toward recent glucose. Standard finger-stick glucometers are for monitoring, not establishing the diagnosis.</p>', ["random-symptoms", "confirmation", "a1c-time", "lab-not-meter"], [q("When is random plasma glucose ≥200 mg/dL diagnostic in the lecture?", "With classic hyperglycemic symptoms or hyperglycemic crisis", ["In every asymptomatic person without confirmation", "Only if A1C is below 5.7%", "Only if it is a standard home meter result"], "The random-glucose criterion includes compatible symptoms/crisis."), q("An asymptomatic patient’s first A1C is 6.6%. What is needed before diagnosis in the absence of unequivocal hyperglycemia?", "Confirmatory repeat testing", ["No confirmation under any circumstances", "A home meter reading replaces laboratory testing", "Islet autoantibodies must be positive in every diabetes type"], "Repeat testing confirms an abnormal diagnostic result when hyperglycemia is not unequivocal.")], [q("A patient has polyuria and polydipsia with laboratory random plasma glucose 236 mg/dL. Does this meet the lecture’s random-glucose criterion?", "Yes: ≥200 mg/dL with classic symptoms", ["No: random glucose must exceed 300 mg/dL", "No: symptoms invalidate the value", "Yes, and it identifies the diabetes type by itself"], "It establishes the glucose criterion; separate clinical/laboratory reasoning identifies type.")])
