      c("Combine clues instead of using one shortcut", '<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Age</td><td>Both types can occur at any age</td></tr><tr><td>Body size</td><td>Overweight/obesity does not exclude T1DM</td></tr><tr><td>Insulin use</td><td>Required in T1DM; may also be needed in T2DM</td></tr><tr><td>DKA</td><td>Strong insulin-deficiency clue, but can occur in T2DM</td></tr></tbody></table><p>No single clinical feature definitively separates the types. Use <strong>islet autoantibodies and C-peptide</strong> when uncertain.</p>', ["age-overlap", "body-size-overlap", "insulin-overlap", "dka-overlap", "classification-tests"], [q("Which statement reliably avoids a classification error?", "Insulin use, obesity, age, or DKA alone cannot definitively classify diabetes", ["Any insulin-treated patient has T1DM", "Obesity excludes T1DM", "DKA never occurs in T2DM"], "Clinical features overlap; use the overall pattern and appropriate tests.")], [q("An adult with obesity develops DKA and is started on insulin. Which conclusion is justified from these facts alone?", "Insulin deficiency needs evaluation; the diabetes type is not established by those features alone", ["Obesity proves T2DM", "Insulin use proves T1DM", "DKA excludes T2DM completely"], "Both obesity and insulin use can occur across types; DKA is suggestive but not exclusive.")]),
      c("When to question an assumed T2DM label", '<p>Consider islet antibody testing with <strong>age &lt;35, BMI &lt;25, unintentional weight loss, ketosis/DKA, marked hyperglycemia, poor response to noninsulin therapy, or rapid insulin requirement</strong>.</p><p>A personal/family autoimmune history increases suspicion. These are prompts to investigate, not mandatory features of T1DM.</p>', ["question-t2-clues", "autoimmune-history"], [q("Which cluster should prompt questioning an assumed T2DM classification?", "Lean younger adult with weight loss, ketosis, and rapid insulin requirement", ["Age alone with no other findings proves T1DM", "Stable glucose and no autoimmune clues exclude all diabetes", "Obesity automatically rules out antibody testing"], "Lean/young onset, catabolic features, ketosis, and rapid progression are suspicion clues.")], [q("A 29-year-old with BMI 22 loses weight and rapidly needs insulin after poor response to a presumed T2DM plan. What diagnostic step is appropriate?", "Evaluate islet autoantibodies and endogenous insulin production", ["Preserve the T2DM label solely because onset was in adulthood", "Diagnose MODY solely from age", "Use insulin requirement alone as final classification"], "The presentation makes autoimmune diabetes clinically possible.")])
