      c("Separate diagnosing diabetes from identifying its type", '<p>Apply the same thresholds: <strong>A1C ≥6.5%, fasting plasma glucose ≥126, 2-hour OGTT ≥200, or random plasma glucose ≥200 mg/dL with classic symptoms/crisis</strong>.</p><p>Confirm if hyperglycemia is not unequivocal. These tests establish diabetes; they do not by themselves establish an autoimmune cause.</p>', ["t1-glucose-criteria", "t1-confirmation"], [q("Do glucose/A1C diagnostic cutoffs alone identify T1DM?", "No; they diagnose diabetes, while classification uses other clinical/lab information", ["Yes; A1C ≥6.5% always means T1DM", "Yes; fasting glucose ≥126 excludes T2DM", "No; glucose tests cannot diagnose diabetes at all"], "Diagnosis and classification are separate decisions.")], [q("A symptomatic patient has random laboratory plasma glucose 248 mg/dL and weight loss. What does the glucose criterion establish?", "Diabetes; additional reasoning/tests establish its type", ["T1DM with no need to consider any other type", "Prediabetes", "No diabetes because the sample is random"], "≥200 mg/dL with classic symptoms meets the diabetes criterion.")]),
      c("C-peptide: your own insulin production", '<p>Proinsulin is cleaved into <strong>insulin + C-peptide</strong>, so C-peptide marks <strong>endogenous insulin production</strong>.</p><p><strong>Low/undetectable:</strong> significant beta-cell loss/insulin deficiency. <strong>Normal/elevated:</strong> preserved secretion, compatible with insulin resistance.</p><p>Interpret with the clinical picture; C-peptide supports classification rather than naming a type by itself.</p>', ["cpeptide-origin", "cpeptide-low", "cpeptide-preserved"], [q("What does a low or undetectable C-peptide suggest?", "Significant loss of endogenous insulin production", ["High endogenous insulin secretion", "Proof of insulin resistance with full compensation", "The amount of carbohydrate eaten last week"], "C-peptide reflects insulin the body produces.")], [q("Diabetes classification is unclear. C-peptide is very low and islet antibodies are present. What interpretation fits?", "Autoimmune insulin deficiency is supported", ["Preserved high insulin secretion is demonstrated", "The values prove uncomplicated insulin resistance alone", "C-peptide directly measures injected insulin dose"], "Low C-peptide supports deficient endogenous insulin; antibodies support autoimmunity.")]),
      c("Islet autoantibodies: evidence of autoimmunity", '<p>The lecture names <strong>insulin autoantibodies, GAD (glutamic acid decarboxylase), IA-2, and ZnT8</strong>.</p><p>They support autoimmune diabetes. Keep them distinct from C-peptide: <strong>antibodies ask why beta cells are failing; C-peptide asks how much endogenous insulin remains</strong>.</p>', ["insulin-antibody", "gad", "ia2", "znt8"], [q("Which set contains the lecture’s islet autoantibodies?", "Insulin, GAD, IA-2, ZnT8", ["A1C, plasma glucose, C-peptide, OGTT", "UACR, eGFR, HDL, triglycerides", "TSH, cortisol, GH, prolactin"], "These four antibodies support autoimmune diabetes classification.")], [q("An adult’s diabetes seems clinically atypical for T2DM. GAD antibodies are detected. What does that finding support?", "Autoimmune diabetes", ["A glucose cutoff that distinguishes prediabetes", "Preserved insulin production regardless of C-peptide", "MODY confirmed by genetic testing"], "GAD is an islet autoantibody, not a glucose or genetic diagnostic test.")])
