      c("Often silent; when symptomatic, recognize hyperglycemia", '<p>T2DM is often <strong>asymptomatic and found on routine labs</strong>.</p><p>Symptoms can include <strong>polyuria, polydipsia, blurred vision, fatigue, and unintentional weight loss</strong>. Their presence does not automatically distinguish T2DM from T1DM.</p>', ["t2-asymptomatic", "t2-classic-symptoms"], [q("Which presentation is common in T2DM?", "Asymptomatic disease discovered on routine laboratory testing", ["Every patient must first present with DKA", "Every patient must have weight loss", "Symptoms alone always distinguish it from T1DM"], "Silent laboratory discovery is common; symptomatic features overlap with T1DM.")], [q("An adult without thirst or urinary complaints has repeat laboratory results meeting diabetes criteria. Does lack of symptoms rule out T2DM?", "No; T2DM is frequently asymptomatic", ["Yes; symptoms are required for every diagnostic test", "Yes; only random glucose can diagnose it", "Yes; A1C is only a symptom score"], "Confirmed laboratory diabetes can occur without classic symptoms.")]),
      c("Additional presentation clues", '<p>Look for <strong>recurrent candidiasis/infections, poor wound healing, acanthosis nigricans, or neuropathic symptoms already present at diagnosis</strong>.</p><p>These support the clinical picture without being diagnostic by themselves.</p>', ["infections-wounds", "acanthosis-neuropathic-presentation"], [q("Which combination is a lecture-listed clue to T2DM?", "Recurrent candidiasis, poor wound healing, and acanthosis nigricans", ["Antibodies alone establish insulin resistance", "An absence of all laboratory abnormalities", "A genetic result confirming MODY"], "Infections, wounds, resistance signs, and neuropathic symptoms may prompt evaluation.")], [q("A patient reports recurrent yeast infections and slow wound healing and has dark thickened neck skin. What is a reasonable evaluation prompted by this pattern?", "Assess for hyperglycemia/T2DM using appropriate laboratory tests", ["Diagnose diabetes from skin appearance alone", "Exclude diabetes because thirst is absent", "Confirm MODY solely from the skin finding"], "The clues warrant glycemic testing; they do not replace diagnostic criteria.")])
